{"name":"Postwar Boom","hex":"#466f97","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"postwar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative spelling of post-war."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/postwar"},{"term":"boom","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make a loud, hollow, resonant sound.","To exclaim with force, to shout, to thunder."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A low-pitched, resonant sound, such as of an explosion.","A rapid expansion or increase."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/boom"}],"entries":[{"title":"Post–World War II economic expansion","blocks":[{"text":"The post–World War II economic expansion, also known as the postwar economic boom or the Golden Age of Capitalism was a broad period of worldwide economic expansion beginning with the aftermath of World War II and ending with the 1973–1975 recession. The United States, the Soviet Union, Australia and Western European and East Asian countries in particular experienced unusually high and sustained growth, together with full employment."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Suburbia_by_David_Shankbone.jpg/330px-Suburbia_by_David_Shankbone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Post%E2%80%93World_War_II_economic_expansion"},{"title":"Post-war","blocks":[{"text":"A post-war or postwar period is the period immediately following the end of a war. The term usually refers to a varying period of time after World War II, which ended in 1945. A post-war period can become an \"interwar period\" or \"interbellum\", when a war between the same or mostly similar parties resumes at a later date. By contrast, a post-war period marks the cessation of armed conflict entirely."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Frenchciviliansburon.jpg/330px-Frenchciviliansburon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Post-war"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Us_unemployment_rates_1950_2005.png/500px-Us_unemployment_rates_1950_2005.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Us_unemployment_rates_1950_2005.png/500px-Us_unemployment_rates_1950_2005.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In the United States, unemployment fluctuated during the 1950s, but dropped steadily during the 1960s.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUs_unemployment_rates_1950_2005.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/GiveUsTheTools.jpg/330px-GiveUsTheTools.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/GiveUsTheTools.jpg/330px-GiveUsTheTools.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Allied war bonds matured during these years, transferring cash from governments to private households.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGiveUsTheTools.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Constructing_the_Stockholm_Metro_in_1957.jpg/500px-Constructing_the_Stockholm_Metro_in_1957.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Constructing_the_Stockholm_Metro_in_1957.jpg/1280px-Constructing_the_Stockholm_Metro_in_1957.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Constructing_the_Stockholm_Metro_in_1957.jpg/500px-Constructing_the_Stockholm_Metro_in_1957.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Many Western governments funded large in